If I insist that the only true red is this particular shade of purple
I happen to like, that's meaningless.  If you insist that the only 
true roleplaying games are those whose rules are whatever it is that 
you assert makes something a roleplaying game, that's also meaningless.  
In both cases it's meaningless because we'd be trying to 
prescriptively define terms that *ALREADY MEAN THINGS* to other 
people.  

Because the meanings of words are fixed by majority opinion, 
insisting that the majority of other English-speaking people in the 
world are wrong about the meanings of words in their own language 
is simply false.  And because most people mean very slightly 
different things when they use most words, "majority opinion" is 
a loose, ambiguous cluster of related meanings that admits of no 
real precision.  

For another example, in some purist sense, "DSL modem" doesn't 
mean anything, because the device is NOT a "modulator/demodulator."
Modem used to be a contraction of "modulator/demodulator" - a term
for a specific type of electronic component that converted analog 
to digital signals and vice versa.  That was what you needed to send
digital signals over the phone lines when the phone network was 
analog. We had to modulate and demodulate in order to get digital
communications over it.  But now the phone network is digital, 
and the device we're using for our DSL connections is properly a 
"network bridge."  But everybody calls it a modem.  The cable 
TV networks are also digital, and the device people are using 
for their Digital-Cable network connections is also a network 
bridge.  But everyone calls it a "Cable modem."

I hated it for the first five years.  Everybody in the world was 
WRONG!  They were printing "modem" on the box of things that were 
not and never had been modems!  They were putting the wrong word 
on sales receipts and billing for it!  When I asked for a "DSL 
network bridge" at the store, I got blank looks from morons who 
didn't know what the hell I was talking about or what the darned 
words even meant!  And these were the jokers who were supposed to 
be knowledgeable about the equipment!!

But you know what?  Eventually I accepted that "modem" is a word 
whose *etymology* refers to a modulator/demodulator, and not a 
contraction which still means that.  It has ceased to be precise 
technical jargon and entered the slang-ridden, messy linguistic 
process which means the majority slowly and sloppily gets to 
decide what the word means and how much ambiguity they want it 
to have, the way they decide what any other word means. These 
days "modem" is a word that means pretty much any device that 
connects two different kinds of networks.

In the same way, "roleplaying game" is not anymore, if it ever has 
been, a precise technical term that means exactly one thing.  You've 
been approaching this from the POV that you are the only one in the
world who knows what the words mean and that the meaning is only 
corrupted by the different usage of it made by others.  This is 
false.  Those others are able to communicate with each other using
this symbol, because their use and expectation of it (that is, their 
language) match each other's - but not, as you've been stridently 
insisting, yours.  There is a widespread consensus about this meaning
that doesn't include you.  And you insisting that the consensus is 
wrong will not hinder in any way the ability of others to communicate 
using that consensus meaning.

You should accept that when other people say "roleplaying game" 
they're talking about a loose, ambiguously-defined genre that 
includes nethack and most other roguelike games.  Because that's 
the rules of linguistic process.  You don't get to say what 
other people mean by their words.  I don't either.  I'm just 
reporting to you majority usage.  

Now, if you have something useful to say about the very restricted 
set of games that you have been thinking of as real roleplaying 
games, you'd better come up with restrictive or new terminology 
whose meaning is not already so widely and strongly agreed to mean 
something else.  In the same way, even network geeks now have 
to use "modulator/demodulator" or "analog modem" to distinguish 
the device when they *don't* mean to include network bridges.

As long as you insist that "roleplaying game" doesn't mean what 
most other English speakers think it means, other people will 
just keep telling you you're wrong.

Because, not to put too fine a point on it, you are.

